data.content:
It works. The key piece is `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` — a built-in policy added in .NET 9. Since your record's constructor parameters are lowercase (`name`, `age`), the C# compiler generates PascalCase properties (`Name`, `Age`) by default, which already match. But `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` is what guarantees PascalCase output regardless of the actual property casing.

Here's the full `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```
